From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Derek Ross <derekross@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 19:19:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: exynos: google: gs201: add the Mali-G710 GPU

Describe the gs201 Mali-G710 for Panthor. Values from Google's GPL-2.0
gs201-gpu.dtsi and gs201.h, checked against the device tree the
bootloader hands over on a Pixel 7 Pro:

 - registers 0x28000000, size 0x480000
 - interrupts GIC SPI 329 (job), 330 (mmu), 328 (gpu)

The GPU clocks belong to the APM firmware: Google's kbase changes them
only through ACPM DVFS requests (domains G3D and G3DL2), which also set
the rail voltage. Until Linux can talk to ACPM on gs201, give the GPU a
fixed-clock placeholder and a single OPP at the rate the bootloader
leaves it at, so devfreq never requests a change. That rate is the boot
level in the bootloader's ECT table: MINMAX_G3D and MINMAX_G3DL2 list
boot = resume = 151 MHz for every ASV group (parsed from the ECT blob in
the device's bl2 partition with the layout from Google's ect_parser.c).

No power-domains yet: the bootloader leaves pd_g3d and pd_embedded_g3d
on (the stock kernel reports both "on" at probe, before anything powers
them), and their on/off sequence needs EL3 calls (PMU writes through
SMC and the D_TZPC restore) that the Exynos power domain driver does
not make. No mali-supply either: the G3D bucks are switched by the PMU
power domain signal and their voltage is set by ACPM.

The node is disabled here and enabled per board.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201.dtsi | 45 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 45 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201.dtsi
index d11f3a9bc..5d5b70a33 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201.dtsi
@@ -131,6 +131,32 @@ usb_ref_clk: clock-usb-ref {
 		clock-output-names = "usb20-ref";
 	};
 
+	/*
+	 * Bring-up placeholder for the GPU clocks. The real rate is owned by
+	 * the APM firmware (ACPM DVFS domains G3D and G3DL2); this only gives
+	 * Panthor a clock and an OPP whose rate matches what the hardware is
+	 * running at, so devfreq never asks for a change.
+	 */
+	gpu_clk: clock-gpu {
+		compatible = "fixed-clock";
+		#clock-cells = <0>;
+		clock-frequency = <151000000>;
+		clock-output-names = "gpu";
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * Single OPP at the boot level from the bootloader's ECT
+	 * (MINMAX_G3D and MINMAX_G3DL2: boot = resume = 151 MHz on every ASV
+	 * group). No opp-microvolt: the rail voltage is set by ACPM.
+	 */
+	gpu_opp_table: opp-table-gpu {
+		compatible = "operating-points-v2";
+
+		opp-151000000 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <151000000>;
+		};
+	};
+
 	reserved-memory {
 		#address-cells = <2>;
 		#size-cells = <1>;
@@ -436,6 +462,25 @@ wakeup-interrupt-controller {
 					     "samsung,exynos7-wakeup-eint";
 			};
 		};
+
+		/*
+		 * No power-domains: the bootloader leaves both G3D power domains
+		 * (pd_g3d, pd_embedded_g3d) on, and nothing in Linux turns them
+		 * off yet. Their on/off sequence needs EL3 calls that the generic
+		 * Exynos power domain driver does not make.
+		 */
+		gpu: gpu@28000000 {
+			compatible = "google,gs201-mali", "arm,mali-valhall-csf";
+			reg = <0x28000000 0x480000>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 329 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				     <GIC_SPI 330 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				     <GIC_SPI 328 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			interrupt-names = "job", "mmu", "gpu";
+			clocks = <&gpu_clk>;
+			clock-names = "core";
+			operating-points-v2 = <&gpu_opp_table>;
+			status = "disabled";
+		};
 	};
 };
 
